  "accessionCode": "CCX-A-13117",
  "description": [
    {
      "value": "<p>Sixteen three-strand lashings gathered into a single carrying hank, plaited wet in one sitting and watched from the first strand to the last. Each is finished with a stopper knot that is worked loose rather than cut, because a wall is taken down and rebuilt far more often than it is built. The lashings tie stacked stone to stone at the corners of a weir and are replaced about twice a year on an outer wall. The hank was given rather than collected, and the record carries the name of the maker and the turn she was holding at the time.</p>",
      "inLanguage": [{ "id": ["core.peerdb.org", "LANGUAGE", "en-GB"] }]
    }
